  1468	  <!-- The Case -->
  1469	  <section class="case-section" id="donor-case">
  1470	    <div class="case-inner">
  1471	      <p class="section-label reveal">The Theological Foundation</p>
  1472	      <h2 class="section-title reveal">Why this organization exists.</h2>
  1473	
  1474	      <div class="scripture-block reveal">
  1475	        <p class="scripture-text">"For even when we were with you, we gave you this rule: The one who is unwilling to work shall not eat."</p>
  1476	        <p class="scripture-ref">2 Thessalonians 3:10 (NIV)</p>
  1477	      </div>
  1478	
  1479	      <p class="case-body reveal">
  1480	        This verse is not a suggestion. The Greek word Paul uses — παραγγέλλω — is a
  1481	        military term for a binding command. Those who are able and willing to work
  1482	        are obligated to work. This has been the position of the Christian tradition
  1483	        from Paul through Luther through Calvin to the present day.
  1484	      </p>
  1485	
  1486	      <p class="case-body reveal">
  1487	        But the mandate has a second edge that the tradition has sometimes overlooked.
  1488	        If those who are willing to work must work, then the community of faith has a
  1489	        corresponding obligation: to ensure that those who are willing have the capacity
  1490	        to do so. When technology displaces a worker from their vocation, the Church
  1491	        does not get to offer thoughts and prayers. The Church is obligated to equip.
  1492	      </p>
  1493	
  1494	      <div class="scripture-block reveal">
  1495	        <p class="scripture-text">"Do you see someone skilled in their work? They will serve before kings; they will not serve before officials of low rank."</p>
  1496	        <p class="scripture-ref">Proverbs 22:29 (NIV)</p>
  1497	      </div>
  1498	
  1499	      <p class="case-body reveal">
  1500	        This is the standard we hold ourselves to. We do not train people to barely
  1501	        qualify for entry-level positions. We train people to stand before kings —
  1502	        to carry credentials that employers actively seek, skills that genuinely
  1503	        transfer, and confidence that comes from genuine mastery.
  1504	      </p>
  1505	
  1506	      <p class="case-body reveal">
  1507	        The Thessalonian Mandate Institute is not a charitable service with religious
  1508	        language applied to it. It is a ministry — an organization that understands
  1509	        workforce retraining as an act of obedience to divine command — and it operates
  1510	        with the theological rigor that obligation demands.
  1511	      </p>
  1512	    </div>
  1513	  </section>
  1514	
  1515	  <!-- Model -->
  1516	  <section class="model-section" id="donor-model">
  1517	    <div class="section-inner">
  1518	      <p class="section-label reveal">Our Model</p>
  1519	      <h2 class="section-title reveal">Lean by design. Powerful by partnership.</h2>
  1520	      <p class="case-body reveal">
  1521	        The Institute does not build curriculum. It builds pipelines. By partnering
  1522	        with IBM, Google, Microsoft, and Khan Academy — platforms that have already
  1523	        invested billions in free workforce training infrastructure — we deliver
  1524	        world-class instruction at zero cost to participants and minimal cost to
  1525	        the organization. Our role is to recruit, assess, support, and place.
  1526	        That is where human ministry happens. That is where your investment goes.
  1527	      </p>
  1528	      <div class="model-grid">
  1529	        <div class="model-card reveal">
  1530	          <div class="model-card-num">01</div>
  1531	          <div class="model-card-title">Recruit and Assess</div>
  1532	          <p class="model-card-text">We find displaced workers through community partnerships, workforce boards, and faith networks. We assess their baseline and match them to the pathway with the highest probability of successful employment.</p>
  1533	        </div>
  1534	        <div class="model-card reveal">
  1535	          <div class="model-card-num">02</div>
  1536	          <div class="model-card-title">Train and Support</div>
  1537	          <p class="model-card-text">Participants train through free platforms — Google, IBM, Microsoft, Khan Academy — in cohorts of 8–15. Weekly check-ins, peer accountability, and navigation support ensure completion rates that free platforms alone cannot achieve.</p>
  1538	        </div>
  1539	        <div class="model-card reveal">
  1540	          <div class="model-card-num">03</div>
  1541	          <div class="model-card-title">Credential and Place</div>
  1542	          <p class="model-card-text">Graduates earn industry-recognized credentials from IBM, Google, and Microsoft. We connect them directly to employer networks, workforce boards, and job fairs — and we advocate for them until they are employed.</p>
  1543	        </div>
  1544	      </div>
  1545	    </div>
  1546	  </section>
  1547	
  1548	  <!-- Founder -->
  1549	  <section class="founder-section" id="donor-founder">
  1550	    <div class="founder-inner">
  1551	      <p class="section-label reveal" style="color:var(--gold)">Our Founder</p>
  1552	      <h2 class="section-title reveal">Built by someone who lived the problem.</h2>
  1553	
  1554	      <p class="founder-text reveal">
  1555	        The Thessalonian Mandate Institute was not founded in a boardroom. It was
  1556	        founded by someone who spent twenty-three years navigating homelessness,
  1557	        instability, and a labor market that stripped away every foothold as fast
  1558	        as it could be found. Eleven of those years were spent as an itinerant
  1559	        street preacher — proclaiming the dignity of labor across the United States
  1560	        from the exact position of someone the economy had left behind.
  1561	      </p>
  1562	
  1563	      <p class="founder-text reveal">
  1564	        The Institute exists because its founder eventually understood that the work
  1565	        mandate of scripture is not a comfort. It is a command — and commands create
  1566	        obligations. The obligation to equip the willing was not being fulfilled.
  1567	        She built the organization that should have existed when she needed it.
  1568	      </p>
  1569	
  1570	      <p class="founder-text reveal">
  1571	        This is a ministry grounded in scripture, tested by experience, and built
  1572	        with the rigor that both demand. The founding documents — Theological
  1573	        Framework, Statement of Faith, and Bylaws — are available to any grantor
  1574	        upon request and reflect the full depth of the doctrinal convictions that
  1575	        drive this work.
  1576	      </p>
  1577	
  1578	      <div class="founder-credential reveal">
  1579	        <div class="founder-name">Rickey Jay Bennett II</div>
  1580	        <div class="founder-title-text">Founder · Minister · Chief Executive<br>The Thessalonian Mandate Institute</div>
  1581	      </div>
  1582	    </div>
  1583	  </section>
  1584	
  1585	  <!-- Give -->
  1586	  <section class="give-section" id="donor-give">
  1587	    <div class="give-inner">
  1588	      <p class="section-label reveal">Support the Mission</p>
  1589	      <h2 class="section-title reveal">Your investment fulfills the mandate.</h2>
  1590	      <p class="case-body reveal">
  1591	        Every dollar given to the Thessalonian Mandate Institute goes directly to
  1592	        the work of equipping displaced workers — case management, cohort support,
  1593	        job placement advocacy, and program operations. No participant ever pays
  1594	        for training. Your giving makes that possible.
  1595	      </p>
  1596	
  1597	      <div class="give-options">
  1598	        <div class="give-card reveal">
  1599	          <div class="give-amount">$50</div>
  1600	          <span class="give-period">Monthly</span>
  1601	          <p class="give-impact">Covers cohort support materials and weekly check-in resources for one participant through their pathway.</p>
  1602	        </div>
  1603	        <div class="give-card reveal">
  1604	          <div class="give-amount">$150</div>
  1605	          <span class="give-period">Monthly</span>
  1606	          <p class="give-impact">Funds full case management support for one participant from intake through job placement.</p>
  1607	        </div>
  1608	        <div class="give-card reveal">
  1609	          <div class="give-amount">$500</div>
  1610	          <span class="give-period">Monthly</span>
  1611	          <p class="give-impact">Sponsors an entire cohort member — covering all support services from enrollment through employment.</p>
  1612	        </div>
  1613	      </div>
  1614	
  1615	      <div class="give-contact reveal">
  1616	        <p>For grant inquiries, major gifts, corporate partnerships, or to request our full organizational documentation including Theological Framework, Statement of Faith, and program curriculum:</p>
  1617	        <a href="mailto:grants@thessalonianmandate.org">grants@thessalonianmandate.org</a>
  1618	      </div>
  1619	    </div>
  1620	  </section>
  1621	
  1622	  <footer class="site-footer">
  1623	    <div class="footer-org">The Thessalonian Mandate Institute</div>
  1624	    <div class="footer-sub">Austin, Texas · 26 U.S.C. § 508(c)(1)(A)</div>
  1625	    <div class="footer-rule"></div>
  1626	    <div class="footer-motto">"The one who is willing to work shall be equipped to work."</div>
  1627	    <div class="footer-legal">
  1628	      Faith-Based Organization · Established under 26 U.S.C. § 508(c)(1)(A)<br>
  1629	      grants@thessalonianmandate.org · Austin, Texas
